{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And your vineyard you shall not glean.</b> That is, when you prune your vineyard, you shall not prune the small berries that are not interwoven in the clusters; they are called עוללות because they are to the rest of the clusters like a child (עולל) to a man. And it is forbidden to the owner of the vineyard to prune them; rather, the poor come and gather them for themselves. And פרט — 'the single grapes' — means the individual grapes that fall and separate from the bunches; these are called פרט. And likewise individual grains of produce Scripture calls פרידות, because they are separated one from another, as it is written (Joel 1): ״עבשו פרדות תחת מגרפותיהן״ — for the prophet prophesies desolation upon the land in grain and in fruit, and therefore he says that when the individual grains that are sown in the ground beneath the soil spoil, it says עבשו, like עפשו, and מגרפותיהן is from the root אגרוף (fist). And the Rabbis use the expression כלל ופרט וכלל ('general and particular and general'), for the כלל is the gathered thing, and the separated part is the פרט." "<b>And in tractate</b> Megillah [they say]: this word דארפט, and it is inverted as if to say דאפרט — that is, it became spoiled and separated. And the Rabbis expounded: in the case of פרט הכרם, if one or two grapes fell, this is פרט; if three fell at once, it is not פרט, and they belong to the owner of the vineyard. And likewise they said: in the field, two stalks are לקט; three are not לקט, and they belong to the owner of the field."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:52.976542900Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}